From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lycaos <caraboune@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 04:30:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] personas-rewire: restore the model-facing persona entry point
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Personas are a documented, fully implemented feature — `[subagents.personas.*]`
in config.toml, `.grok/personas/*.toml`, `~/.grok/personas/*.toml`, a
`/personas` modal, resolution with model / reasoning-effort / isolation
defaults, and injection of the persona instructions as a `<system-reminder>`
in the child conversation. Upstream ships it with both ends cut:

  - `PromptContext::format_personas_section` returns `None` unconditionally,
    so the `<personas>` catalog never reaches the model;
  - `render_io_summary` has no caller in the tree and the session is handed
    `Vec::new()` for `persona_summaries`, so the catalog would be empty anyway;
  - the `task` tool builds its spawn request with a hardcoded `persona: None`
    because the parameter was dropped from the model-facing schema.

Everything between those points is intact, including the fail-closed guard
that aborts a spawn when a persona name does not resolve.

This reconnects the three: the session builds its catalog from the effective
personas map (sorted by name — the system prompt is cached, so a HashMap's
iteration order must not leak into it), `format_personas_section` renders the
block, and `TaskToolInput` advertises `persona` again and forwards it to
subagent resolution with the same sentinel handling as `model`. Unlike
`model`, it survives a resume: the resume footer asks for the source's
persona back and resume-identity validation compares them.

`.grok/` joins .gitignore for the same reason the feature is worth having.
Personas resolve from a project scope too (`<cwd>/.grok/personas/*.toml`),
which puts user-authored persona text inside whichever repository the session
runs in — this fork included, where nothing under `.grok/` is tracked and a
stray `git add -A` would publish it. Prompt overrides and presets never had
this exposure: they live under `$GROK_HOME`, outside every git tree.

Critical in the queue for the same reason as prompts-modal: upstream keeps
shipping the stub, so a silent skip on conflict would restore the amputation.
The `personas-wired` contract is what proves the entry point is still open.

Gork-Patch-Id: personas-rewire
Gork-Risk: low
